      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Why are Woodway treadmills so expensive?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Because the running surface is a slat belt on ball bearings rather than a rubber belt sliding on a deck. That means dozens of slats, a toothed drive system and rows of bearings instead of one moulded loop, on a steel frame built for a commercial duty cycle. The design removes friction, which removes lubrication, deck wear and the gradual rise in motor load that kills conventional treadmills — and it costs what that engineering costs.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Do Woodway treadmills need lubrication?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "No. There is no sliding contact between a belt and a deck, so there is nothing to lubricate — not at a longer interval, but never. Since skipped lubrication is the single most common cause of expensive treadmill failure, removing the requirement removes the failure mode. Bearings and slats are the wear items instead, and both are replaceable.",
          },
