    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Almost every treadmill problem this site writes about — lubrication, deck wear, belt
          slip, friction killing a motor — is a consequence of one design decision: a rubber belt
          dragging across a board. Woodway does not make that decision, and the price is what
          not making it costs.
        </p>

        <div id="slat-belt" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">1. The Slat Belt: What Woodway Actually Made</h2>
<p>
            A conventional treadmill is a continuous rubber belt sliding over a fixed wooden deck.
            The friction between those two surfaces is the enemy of the entire machine. It is why
            you lubricate, it is what wears the deck smooth, it is what makes the motor work harder
            every year, and it is the cause of most expensive treadmill failures. Our
            <a href="/how-long-do-treadmill-motors-last/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">guide to motor lifespan</a>
            is essentially a long description of that one problem.
          </p>
          <p>
            Woodway replaced the sliding contact with a rolling one. The running surface is made of
            individual slats — traditionally rubber-coated, mounted on a toothed transport system
            that rides on rows of ball bearings inside the frame rails. Nothing slides against
            anything. The belt rolls.
          </p>
          <p>
            Three things follow, and they are the entire product.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>There is no lubrication.</strong> Not a longer interval — none. The maintenance
            routine that this site covers across half a dozen guides does not apply, because there
            is no sliding interface to lubricate.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>There is no deck to wear out.</strong> A conventional deck has a low-friction
            coating that polishes away over years, after which friction climbs and the motor pays
            for it. There is no coating here to lose, so the machine does not get progressively
            harder on itself with age the way a belt-and-deck treadmill does.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The surface feels different underfoot.</strong> Firmer and more consistent than
            a cushioned deck, with a slight give from the slats themselves. People coming from a
            soft consumer treadmill often describe it as hard at first and then stop noticing,
            because it is closer to running on ground than a sprung deck is. Physiotherapists tend
            to like it for exactly that reason.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="the-range" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">2. The Range, Briefly</h2>
<p>
            Woodway’s catalogue is aimed at institutions, so the model names mean little to a
            consumer. The useful division is by what drives the belt.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Motorised slat treadmills</strong> are the core of the business — the machines
            you find in hospital gait labs, professional team facilities, military rehabilitation
            units and serious commercial gyms. Within that group the differences are mostly deck
            length, incline range, console sophistication and whether the machine includes force
            or gait measurement. Some models offer decline as well as incline, which is unusual and
            genuinely useful for anyone training for downhill running or rehabilitating an eccentric
            loading injury.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The Curve</strong> is the non-motorised one, and it is the model most people
            outside institutions have actually stood on. The slat surface is curved, there is no
            motor and no power cable, and the belt moves because you do — stepping further forward
            on the curve accelerates it, drifting back slows it. It became widely known through
            competitive fitness gyms and is now the reference point for the entire
            <a href="/curved-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">curved treadmill category</a>.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Self-powered and energy-recovering variants</strong> sit alongside these,
            including machines that generate rather than consume electricity. These are largely a
            facility-level consideration rather than a home one.
          </p>
          <p>
            For a home buyer, the practical choice is almost always between a used motorised slat
            machine and a Curve, and those two are so different in purpose that the decision makes
            itself once you know whether you want to run steadily or sprint.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="why-it-costs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. Why It Costs What It Costs</h2>
<p>
            A Woodway is priced like a piece of capital equipment because that is what it is sold
            as. New, these machines sit an order of magnitude above a good consumer treadmill, and
            the top of the range is genuinely in the territory of a used car.
          </p>
          <p>
            The cost is not margin on a badge. A slat belt has dozens of individual slats, a
            toothed drive system and rows of bearings, against a competitor’s single moulded rubber
            loop. The frames are steel and heavy in a way that consumer machines are not, because
            they are built to survive a twenty-four-hour commercial duty cycle rather than forty
            minutes a day in a spare room. The whole machine is engineered around a service life
            that is measured in decades and in tens of thousands of miles of belt travel.
          </p>
          <p>
            Whether that is good value depends entirely on the denominator. For a gym running a
            machine sixteen hours a day, the arithmetic is straightforward: one Woodway outlasts
            several consumer treadmills, needs no deck replacements and almost no scheduled
            servicing, and does not have to be taken out of service. Commercial buyers do this
            calculation carefully and keep arriving at the same answer.
          </p>
          <p>
            For a home user doing four hours a week, the denominator is far smaller and the
            arithmetic mostly does not work. A
            <a href="/treadmills-over-2500/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">good $3,000 home treadmill</a>
            will comfortably outlast a home user’s interest in it, and buying thirty years of
            durability for a machine you will move house with twice is spending money on a problem
            you do not have. That is the honest position, and it is why the next section is about
            the used market.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="maintenance" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. The Maintenance Case</h2>
<p>
            This is where a Woodway is genuinely, measurably different from anything else, and it
            is the part that appeals most to people who have owned a treadmill before.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>No lubrication, permanently.</strong> Every guide on this site about silicone,
            intervals, application method and what happens when you skip it becomes irrelevant.
            That is not a small convenience — skipped lubrication is the single biggest cause of
            expensive treadmill failure, and removing the possibility removes the failure.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>No deck flipping or replacement.</strong> On a conventional machine the deck is
            a consumable with a low-friction face that wears out. Here there is no such surface.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Belt tension and tracking are still a thing</strong>, but they behave
            differently and are adjusted differently from a rubber belt. This is not a
            you-and-a-hex-key job in the same casual way.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Bearings and slats are the wear items.</strong> They last a very long time and
            they are replaceable, which is the other half of the durability argument — a Woodway is
            designed to be rebuilt rather than discarded. That is also why the used market exists in
            a way it does not for consumer machines.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Service is specialised.</strong> The flip side of all of the above. Your local
            treadmill repair technician who is excellent with a Sole or a NordicTrack may have never
            opened a slat machine, and parts come through the dealer network. Factor the availability
            of a technician into a purchase, particularly a used one, because a cheap machine you
            cannot get serviced is not cheap.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="curve-vs-motorised" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. Curve vs Motorised: A Real Decision</h2>
<p>
            These are not two versions of the same product. They do different jobs and suit
            different training.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The Curve is a sprint and interval machine.</strong> Because you drive it, the
            effort at any given pace is markedly higher than on a motorised belt, and the machine
            responds instantly to a change of intent — no lag while a motor spins up, which is
            exactly what makes it good for short, hard efforts. It is also excellent for pushing
            work and for athletes who want a surface with no electronics between intention and
            speed.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The Curve is a poor steady-state machine.</strong> Holding a precise pace for
            forty minutes is harder than on a motorised belt because there is nothing enforcing it
            — the pace drifts with your attention. Anyone doing long, even-paced running is better
            served by a motor, and most walkers will find a Curve unnecessarily hard work.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The motorised machines are for everything else</strong>, and they are where the
            incline, decline, programmable protocols and measurement capability live. If the goal is
            structured running training, rehabilitation, or any protocol requiring a controlled and
            repeatable speed, the motor is not optional.
          </p>
          <p>
            One practical note: the Curve has no power requirement at all, which makes it usable in
            a garage without a suitable circuit and removes
            <a href="/treadmill-electricity-usage/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">running electricity costs</a>
            entirely. It is also extremely heavy, so the absence of a cable does not make it
            portable in any meaningful sense.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="buying-used" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. The Used Market Is the Real Route</h2>
<p>
            This is the practical answer for almost every individual who wants a Woodway, and the
            reason is structural rather than opportunistic. Gyms, clinics, universities and teams
            buy these machines, run them hard for years, and then replace them on a capital cycle
            that has more to do with accounting and refurbishment schedules than with the machine
            being finished. A Woodway leaving a facility frequently has most of its life ahead of
            it.
          </p>
          <p>
            That produces something unusual: a durable-goods market where the secondhand price is a
            small fraction of new, and where the depreciation has very little to do with remaining
            usefulness. Our
            <a href="/new-vs-used-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">new versus used analysis</a>
            makes the general case; on this brand it is considerably stronger than on any consumer
            machine, because the thing you are buying is the frame and the mechanism, and both
            survive commercial use well.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>What to check, beyond the
            <a href="/used-treadmill-buying-checklist/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">standard inspection</a>:</strong>
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Slat condition.</strong> Look along the running surface for cracked, chipped or
            missing slat facings, and for any slat sitting proud of its neighbours. Individual
            slats are replaceable, but a surface with widespread damage is a bill.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Bearing noise.</strong> Run it and listen. The mechanism should be smooth and
            consistent. A rhythmic knock or a rumble that changes with speed points at the bearing
            system, which is the expensive end of the machine.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Console and electronics.</strong> Frequently the weakest and most dated part of
            an older commercial machine, and the hardest to source parts for. Test every function.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Power requirements.</strong> Some commercial machines expect a supply a
            domestic room does not have. Establish exactly what the unit needs before you buy, not
            after it is in your garage.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Weight and access.</strong> These machines are extremely heavy and do not fold,
            tilt or come apart conveniently. Measure the route, price professional movers, and treat
            that as part of the purchase price rather than a detail.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. Who Should Actually Buy One</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Buy new if you are a facility.</strong> Gym, clinic, team, university,
            corporate space. The durability arithmetic works, the service relationship has value,
            and downtime costs you something.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Buy used if you are a serious individual with space and patience.</strong> A
            runner logging real weekly mileage indoors, someone with a joint history who benefits
            from the surface, or a home gym owner who would rather buy one machine permanently than
            three machines in sequence. You need a garage or basement, a way to move 400 lbs or
            more, and a technician within reach.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Buy a Curve if you sprint.</strong> Short hard efforts, pushing work, and
            training where instant response matters. It is a specialist tool and an excellent one.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Do not buy one if you are a normal home user.</strong> This is the honest
            conclusion and it applies to most people reading this page. If you run three or four
            times a week and want a good machine, a
            <a href="/best-treadmills-for-running/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">quality consumer running treadmill</a>
            does the job for a fraction of the money and will outlast your enthusiasm for it. The
            Woodway advantages — thirty-year frames, commercial duty cycles, rebuildability — are
            answers to institutional problems.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>And do not buy one because you hate lubricating a treadmill.</strong> It is a
            real benefit and it is not worth several thousand dollars. Lubrication is
            <a href="/treadmill-belt-lubrication/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">ten minutes every few months</a>.
            If that is the deciding factor, the decision has gone wrong somewhere earlier.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="alternatives" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. The Alternatives at a Third of the Price</h2>
<p>
            If what appeals is the durability rather than the badge, there are sensible steps down.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Other slat-belt machines.</strong> Woodway pioneered the design but no longer
            has it to itself, and there are now slat treadmills at meaningfully lower prices from
            other manufacturers. They are worth cross-shopping, with the caveat that slat quality
            and bearing quality are exactly where cost gets taken out, and those are the components
            you were buying the design for.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Curved manual machines from competitive fitness brands.</strong> Several
            manufacturers make curved non-motorised treadmills at a fraction of a Curve’s price.
            They are heavier-feeling and less refined, and for most home users they do the same job.
            The
            <a href="/curved-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">curved treadmill guide</a>
            works through what separates them.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Genuine commercial belt-and-deck treadmills.</strong> A used
            <a href="/life-fitness/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Life Fitness</a> or
            <a href="/matrix-fitness/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Matrix</a> from a gym
            refit gives you a heavy frame, a long deck and commercial reliability for considerably
            less. You are back to lubricating a deck, but you are also back to a machine any
            technician can service and any supplier can stock parts for. Our
            <a href="/commercial-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">commercial treadmill guide</a>
            covers what to look for.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>A very good consumer machine.</strong> For most people this is the right answer
            and always was. Spend the money on deck length and a real motor, follow the maintenance
            schedule, and the machine will outlast the reason you bought it.
          </p>
        </div>
