      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why are Woodway treadmills so expensive?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Because the running surface is a slat belt on ball bearings rather than a rubber belt sliding on a deck. That means dozens of slats, a toothed drive system and rows of bearings instead of one moulded loop, on a steel frame built for a commercial duty cycle. The design removes friction, which removes lubrication, deck wear and the gradual rise in motor load that kills conventional treadmills — and it costs what that engineering costs.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do Woodway treadmills need lubrication?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. There is no sliding contact between a belt and a deck, so there is nothing to lubricate — not at a longer interval, but never. Since skipped lubrication is the single most common cause of expensive treadmill failure, removing the requirement removes the failure mode. Bearings and slats are the wear items instead, and both are replaceable.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a Woodway Curve worth it for home use?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For sprint and interval training, it is an excellent tool and the response is instant because there is no motor to spin up. For steady-state running it is worse than a motorised belt, since nothing enforces the pace and it drifts with your attention. It also needs no power at all, which suits a garage, but it is extremely heavy and does not move easily.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does a Woodway treadmill last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Woodway publishes belt life figures in the tens of thousands of miles and the machines are designed to be rebuilt rather than replaced, which is why so many reach the used market still working after years of commercial service. We have not run one to destruction ourselves, so treat the published durability figures as the manufacturer's claims — but the secondhand market's existence is reasonable independent evidence.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I buy a used Woodway treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For an individual, it is usually the only sensible route. Gyms and clinics replace them on capital cycles rather than because they are worn out, so secondhand units often have most of their life left at a small fraction of new. Check slat condition, listen for bearing noise, test every console function, confirm the power requirement suits a domestic room, and budget for professional movers.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the difference between a slat belt and a normal treadmill belt?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A normal belt is one continuous rubber loop sliding across a fixed deck, and that sliding friction causes deck wear, rising motor load and the need for lubrication. A slat belt is many individual slats on a bearing system, so the surface rolls rather than slides. The result is firmer underfoot, more consistent over years, and free of the maintenance that friction creates.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes Buying a Woodway</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">These machines are bought well by institutions and badly by individuals, and the pattern of error is consistent.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying commercial durability you will never use</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A thirty-year frame and a commercial duty cycle are answers to a gym’s problem, not a spare room’s. If you run four hours a week, a good consumer treadmill will outlast your interest in it for a fraction of the money. Work out your actual weekly hours before paying for a machine engineered around sixteen-hour days.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Not confirming the power supply before delivery</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Some commercial machines expect a supply a domestic room does not have, and this is discovered far too often after 400 lbs of treadmill is already in the garage. Establish the exact electrical requirement from the model and serial number before money changes hands, and get an electrician to confirm the circuit if there is any doubt.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Underestimating the weight and the move</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">These do not fold, do not tilt helpfully and do not come apart for convenience. Measure the full route including stairwell turns, doorway widths and floor loading, and price professional movers as part of the purchase rather than as an afterthought. A bargain you cannot get into the building is not a bargain.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a Curve for steady-state running</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">The Curve is superb for sprints and intervals and mediocre for holding an even pace, because nothing enforces the speed and it drifts as you tire or stop concentrating. Walkers in particular find it unnecessarily hard work. If most of your training is long and even-paced, you want a motor, and buying the wrong Woodway is an expensive way to learn that.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

